      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07 issued by NMO (UK) under Scheme A.\n\nKey information:\n- Certificate number: R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\n- OIML Issuing Authority: NMO, Stanton Avenue, Teddington, TW11 0JZ, United Kingdom\n- Person responsible: Mannie Panesar – Head of Technical Services\n- Applicant: Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG, 37070 Goettingen, Germany\n- Manufacturer: The applicant (so same as applicant)\n- Certified type: Models EVO1..., VIS1..., SPO1..., LAB1... (many models)\n- Recommendation: OIML R 76, Edition 2006\n- Accuracy classes: II and III\n- Issue date: 11 May 2018\n- Signature: H. Bokota, M Bokota, Technical Manager — interesting, the signature says \"H. Bokota\" but the printed name is \"M Bokota\". The person who signs is M Bokota, Technical Manager. Person responsible listed: Mannie Panesar – Head of Technical Services. The signing is \"For and on behalf of the Head of Technical Services\".\n\n- Test reports: No. P02276 dated 11 May 2018, 30 pages, role: type_evaluation_report\n- Documentation file: No. P02276-D dated 11 May 2018, role: documentation_file\n- Revision history: Revision 0, 11/05/2018, \"OIML Certificate first issued.\"\n\nPage total: 5 pages (Page 2 of 5, Page 3 of 5).\n\nIssuing authority details:\n- name: NMO\n- address_lines: Stanton Avenue, Teddington, TW11 0JZ, United Kingdom\n- person_responsible: Mannie Panesar\n- person_title: Head of Technical Services\n- phone: +44 (0) 20 8943 7272\n- fax: +44 (0) 20 8943 7270 — not in schema, could omit or include; schema has phone, email, website. Fax not listed; omit.\n- website: www.gov.uk/government/organisations/office-for-product-safety-and-standards\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present? There's \"0135\" next to UKAS logo — that's the UKAS number, not an OIML issuer ID. Omit oiml_issuer_id.\n\nCertificate:\n- number: R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07\n- scheme: A\n- project_number: Could \"GB1\" be part? Not clearly. The number format R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07. Project number... The test report is P02276. Not a project number on the certificate itself. Omit project_number.\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\n- date_issued: 2018-05-11\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present\n\nCertified type:\n- category: non-automatic weighing instrument → \"Weighing instrument\" — the schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\". For R76 it's non-automatic weighing instrument. I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n- type_designations: all the model identifiers listed:\n  EVO1S1N3CI-C, EVO1S1CI-C, EVO1Y1N3CI-C, EVO1Y1CI-C, EVO1X1N3CI-C, EVO1X1CI-C, EVO1X2N3CI-C, EVO1X2CI-C, EVO1X3N3CI-C, EVO1X3CI-C, EVO1X4N3CI-C, EVO1X4CI-C, VIS1Y1M3CI-C, VIS1Y1CI-C, VIS1Y2M3CI-C, VIS1Y2CI-C, VIS1Y3M3CI-C, VIS1Y3CI-C, VIS1Y4M3CI-C, VIS1Y4CI-C, VIS1X1M3CI-C, VIS1X1CI-C, VIS1X2M3CI-C, VIS1X2CI-C, VIS1X3M3CI-C, VIS1X3CI-C, VIS1X4M3CI-C, VIS1X4CI-C, SPO1S1N3CI-C, SPO1S1CI-C, LAB1X1N3CI-C, LAB1X1CI-C, LAB1X2N3CI-C, LAB1X2CI-C, LAB1X3N3CI-C, LAB1X3CI-C, LAB1X4N3CI-C, LAB1X4CI-C\n\n  That's 38 models (28 EV + 10 HD).\n\n- module_designation: null / not applicable (this is a complete weighing instrument, not a module). Omit or \"Not applicable\". The instruments are complete — the certificate is for instruments, not modules. I'll omit since not mentioned. Actually, the schema says \"module_designation: 'Analog load cell' | 'Not applicable' | null\". For a complete instrument, \"Not applicable\" seems right. But rule 8 says don't fabricate; the cert doesn't mention module designation. I'll omit it... Hmm. The schema offers null as an option. I'll just omit. Actually I could set it to null. I'll omit the field to be safe. Hmm, but including \"module_designation\": null is fine too. I'll include it as null? Rule 8: if a field is not present, omit. I'll omit.\n\n- description: \"Self-indicating, single-interval, non-automatic weighing instruments designed to be used in paint mixing applications. The family comprises the PMA-EV (Class II) and the PMA-HD (Class III) types... The instrument shall not be used for direct sales to the public.\" Also construction: plastic construction, stainless steel load receptor, plastic display head.\n\nModel family:\n- family_name: The types are PMA-EV and PMA-HD. So there are two type families. model_family.family_name could be null, or \"PMA-EV / PMA-HD\". Hmm. The cert covers two types: PMA-EV (Class II) and PMA-HD (Class III). The models belong to one or the other.\n\n  How to model? models[] entries could have attributes like {\"type\": \"PMA-EV\", \"accuracy_class\": \"II\"}. But we don't know per-model which type they belong to... Actually we do: EVO1* and VIS1* are PMA-EV; SPO1* and LAB1* are PMA-HD. Based on \"Type PMA-EV:\" listing and \"Type PMA-HD:\" listing.\n\n  We could encode each model with attribute \"instrument_type\" and \"accuracy_class\".\n\n  family_name: null (no explicit single family name; could be \"PMA\" paint mixing family?). The description says \"The family comprises of the PMA-EV (Class II) and the PMA-HD (Class III) types\". So there's a family with two types. family_name could be null since no explicit family name given. I'll set null.\n\n- models[]: one entry per variant. 38 entries is a lot but let's list them. Each with model_id and label maybe. attributes: instrument_type (PMA-EV / PMA-HD), environment (S: standard, X: Ex-zone 1, Y: Ex-zone 2), RJ-45 cable length (B digit: for A=S and EVO1Y models B=1; for others B ∈ 1,2,3,4 → cable 0m/10m/20m/30m).\n\nWait — careful: \"For A =S and EVO1Y models, B = 1 and for all other models B = 1, 2, 3, or 4 depending on supplied RJ-45 cable lengths of: no cable, 10m, 20m, or 30m respectively.\"\n\nHmm, \"EVO1Y\" models — but the model naming says A = S, X, or Y. For A = S and... wait it says \"EVO1Y models\" but presumably it means \"Y models\"? Actually the text: \"For A =S and EVO1Y models, B = 1\". Hmm, that's odd — maybe Ex models come with fixed cable. But VIS1Y models exist with M3 (B=1? VIS1Y1M3CI-C: A=Y, B=1, M3 = commercial affix?). Let me parse model name composition: \"EVO1 ABC\" where A, B, C. E.g., EVO1S1N3CI-C: A=S, B=1, C=N3CI-C. VIS1Y1M3CI-C: A=Y, B=1, C=M3CI-C. So the M3/N3 are commercial name affixes (C placeholder).\n\nSo for all Y models listed, B=1 (e.g., VIS1Y1..., EVO1Y1...). That matches \"For A = S and EVO1Y models, B = 1\" — hmm but VIS1Y also only have B=1. The text says \"EVO1Y\" but the data shows all Y models have B=1. Whatever — I shouldn't over-interpret; just record the models as listed.\n\nI'll keep model attributes minimal: instrument_type and maybe environment_code. Actually, to avoid fabrication, I can include attributes: {\"instrument_type\": \"PMA-EV\", \"prefix\": \"EVO1\"}. Hmm, might be over-engineering. The schema says models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. Including instrument_type per model is well-supported by the document (Type PMA-EV listing). I'll include instrument_type and accuracy_class (II for PMA-EV, III for PMA-HD).\n\nFor 38 models, list each:\n\nPMA-EV (Class II):\n1. EVO1S1N3CI-C\n2. EVO1S1CI-C\n3. EVO1Y1N3CI-C\n4. EVO1Y1CI-C\n5. EVO1X1N3CI-C\n6. EVO1X1CI-C\n7. EVO1X2N3CI-C\n8. EVO1X2CI-C\n9. EVO1X3N3CI-C\n10. EVO1X3CI-C\n11. EVO1X4N3CI-C\n12. EVO1X4CI-C\n13. VIS1Y1M3CI-C\n14. VIS1Y1CI-C\n15. VIS1Y2M3CI-C\n16. VIS1Y2CI-C\n17. VIS1Y3M3CI-C\n18. VIS1Y3CI-C\n19. VIS1Y4M3CI-C\n20. VIS1Y4CI-C\n21. VIS1X1M3CI-C\n22. VIS1X1CI-C\n23. VIS1X2M3CI-C\n24. VIS1X2CI-C\n25. VIS1X3M3CI-C\n26. VIS1X3CI-C\n27. VIS1X4M3CI-C\n28. VIS1X4CI-C\n\nPMA-HD (Class III):\n29. SPO1S1N3CI-C\n30. SPO1S1CI-C\n31. LAB1X1N3CI-C\n32. LAB1X1CI-C\n33. LAB1X2N3CI-C\n34. LAB1X2CI-C\n35. LAB1X3N3CI-C\n36. LAB1X3CI-C\n37. LAB1X4N3CI-C\n38. LAB1X4CI-C\n\nCharacteristics:\n- type_level: any whole-cert attributes? Accuracy class II and III — that's in recommendation.accuracy_classes. Construction items — could be characteristics but they're descriptive. \"single-interval\" is a characteristic. Maybe include \"interval_type\": \"single-interval\"? Could add. Also \"not for direct sales to the public\" is a restriction, not characteristic.\n\n  I could add type_level:\n  - \"intervals\": \"single-interval\"\n  - \"operation\": \"self-indicating\"\n  - \"application\": \"paint mixing\"\n  \n  Hmm, these are more description. Maybe keep description field and skip type_level. But including a couple of structured ones is nice. I'll keep it minimal: maybe \"class\" is already in recommendation. I'll skip type_level or include single_interval: true? Let me think — the schema wants labels like maximum_capacity etc. I'll include:\n  - \"instrument_class\": \"self-indicating, single-interval\" — no, avoid. I'll just omit type_level, or include \"single_interval\": {value: true}? Booleans not in spec (number|string|range). I'll omit type_level entirely.\n\n  Actually maybe config_level: cable length is a config axis! \"B = 1, 2, 3, or 4 depending on supplied RJ-45 cable lengths of: no cable, 10m, 20m, or 30m respectively.\" That's a configuration axis: RJ-45 cable length per B digit. That could be config_level:\n  ```\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"rj45_cable_length\",\n    \"axis\": \"model name digit B\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"B=1\", \"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"B=2\", \"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"B=3\", \"value\": 20, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"B=4\", \"value\": 30, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"}\n    ]\n  }\n  ```\n  Wait: \"no cable, 10m, 20m, or 30m respectively\" — no cable = no cable (null or 0?). \"no cable\" → value: \"no cable\" or 0? I'd use \"no cable\" as string, or 0 m. Hmm. I'll use \"no cable\" string for B=1... Actually safer: value: 0? That's interpretation. The document says \"no cable\". I'll record value: \"no cable\" with unit_symbol null? But schema wants value object shape with unit_symbol possibly null. For string values, unit_symbol can be null. Hmm, but \"no cable\" means not connected, so 0 m could be a fabrication. I'll use \"no cable\".\n\n  Also environment axis: A digit: S = standard environments, X = Ex-zone 1 explosive environments, Y = Ex-zone 2 explosive environments. That's config_level too:\n  ```\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"operating_environment\",\n    \"axis\": \"model name letter A\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"A=S\", \"value\": \"standard environments\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"A=X\", \"value\": \"Ex-zone 1 explosive environments\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"A=Y\", \"value\": \"Ex-zone 2 explosive environments\"}\n    ]\n  }\n  ```\n  \n  These are reasonable. But is config_level supposed to be numeric characteristics? Rule 5 says \"When attributes vary by configuration (not by model — e.g. 2-sensor-row vs 4-sensor-row setups; static vs dynamic weighing)\". The A/B digits are model-name-encoded configuration. Since these are encoded in the model name, they could also go into model attributes. I think putting them in config_level is defensible and useful. Alternatively put in each model's attributes. Doing both is redundant. I'll put them in config_level since the axis is a naming dimension, and also add per-model attributes minimally? Hmm.\n\n  Let me decide: models[] entries get attributes {\"instrument_type\": \"PMA-EV\", \"accuracy_class\": \"II\", \"name_letter_A\": \"S\", \"name_digit_B\": 1}. That's per-model encoding. And config_level gets the decoding of the axes (A → environment, B → cable length). This seems comprehensive without contradiction.\n\n  Actually maybe that's too much. Let me keep models' attributes to instrument_type and accuracy_class, and use config_level for the A/B decoding. That's clean.\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R76\"\n- edition: 2006\n- amendment: null (omit)\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"II\", \"III\"]\n\nTest reports:\n- {id: \"P02276\", date: \"2018-05-11\", pages: 30, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"P02276-D\", date: \"2018-05-11\", role: \"documentation_file\"} — pages not given, omit pages.\n\nRevision history:\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2018-05-11\", changes: \"OIML Certificate first issued.\"}\n\nDate format: The schema doesn't specify format. Use ISO \"2018-05-11\". For revision date \"11/05/2018\" → \"2018-05-11\".\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"NMO\" — full name? The footer says \"NMO is part of the Office for Product Safety and Standards within the Department for Business, Energy & Industrial Strategy\". The name given is NMO. I'll use \"NMO\".\n- address_lines: [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Mannie Panesar\"\n- person_title: \"Head of Technical Services\"\n- phone: \"+44 (0) 20 8943 7272\"\n- website: \"www.gov.uk/government/organisations/office-for-product-safety-and-standards\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not given. Omit.\n\nNote: \"Person responsible: Mannie Panesar – Head of Technical Services\" — split into person/title. Also signature block: \"M Bokota, Technical Manager, For and on behalf of the Head of Technical Services\". The schema person_responsible is the listed responsible person: Mannie Panesar. The signer is different; no schema field for signer. Keep Mannie Panesar.\n\nApplicants:\n- name: \"Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG\"\n- address_lines: [\"37070 Goettingen\", \"Germany\"]\n\nManufacturers:\n- \"The applicant\" — meaning same as applicant. Should I create a manufacturers entry duplicating applicant? The cert says \"Manufacturer: The applicant\". I could add a manufacturers[] entry with the same name/address, or note. Rule 12 mentions layouts. I'll add manufacturers entry with same details (resolving \"The applicant\"). Hmm, or omit since it's just a reference. I think resolving it is more useful: manufacturers: [{name: \"Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG\", address_lines: [...]}]. But that is technically an inference — a safe one, since \"The applicant\" directly refers to the applicant. I'll resolve it.\n\nCertificate.project_number: not present. Omit.\n\ncertificate.page_total: 5. The fragment shows \"Page 2 of 5 pages\" and \"Page 3 of 5\". Total is 5.\n\ncertificate.scheme: \"A\".\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"2018-05-11\".\n\ncertificate.member_state: \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\".\n\ncertified_type.category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". The examples list categories; I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" (R76 instruments). Good.\n\ncertified_type.description: Combine: \"Self-indicating, single-interval, non-automatic weighing instruments designed to be used in paint mixing applications. The family comprises the PMA-EV (Class II) and the PMA-HD (Class III) types. The instrument shall not be used for direct sales to the public. Construction: plastic construction, stainless steel load receptor, plastic display head.\"\n\nHmm, construction could be a components/characteristics thing. I'll keep in description, plus maybe config-level. Construction items: \"Plastic construction\", \"Stainless steel load receptor\", \"Plastic display head\" — these are construction features. Could add type_level characteristics like \"construction\": value [\"Plastic construction\", ...]? Lists aren't standard. Keep in description.\n\ncertified_type.type_designations: also should include PMA-EV and PMA-HD type names? They're type names (families). I'll include them too: \"PMA-EV\", \"PMA-HD\". The type_designations field says \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". So include both family type names and 38 models. Hmm, that makes 40 entries. That's fine but maybe put PMA-EV/PMA-HD in model_family as... they're two types. Let me include them in type_designations since they're \"Type PMA-EV\" / \"Type PMA-HD\" designations.\n\nNow, model_family: family_name — is there a single family name? \"The family comprises of the PMA-EV (Class II) and the PMA-HD (Class III) types\" — so family name not explicitly given; the composition figure shows Type PMA-EV and Type PMA-HD. family_name: null.\n\nmodels[]: 38 entries with model_id and attributes. Do I need \"label\"? Optional. I'll skip label or use the type as label. I'll include attributes: {\"instrument_type\": \"PMA-EV\", \"accuracy_class\": \"II\"}.\n\nShould I include the A/B digits in attributes? Let me add \"environment_code\" and \"cable_code\"? This is derived info but directly from model naming. I'll add:\n- \"name_letter_A\": \"S\"|\"X\"|\"Y\" — hmm, over-detailed. Let me just add \"environment\": \"standard\" | \"Ex-zone 1\" | \"Ex-zone 2\"? That's interpretation of the code, well-supported. And \"rj45_cable_length\"? For X models, B varies (1-4) so cable varies per model; e.g., EVO1X2CI-C → B=2 → 10m. For S models B=1 always → no cable. For EVO1Y → B=1. For VIS1Y → B=1 (all listed Y models have B=1). For VIS1X → B varies.\n\nAdding cable length per model is derivable: B digit maps to cable length. It's a nice touch but risks clutter. I'll include \"rj45_cable_length\" in model attributes only where derivable, or put the mapping in config_level and the codes in model attributes.\n\nDecision: \n- models[].attributes: {\"instrument_type\": ..., \"accuracy_class\": ..., \"environment_code\": \"S\"|\"X\"|\"Y\", \"cable_code\": 1|2|3|4}\n- config_level: two entries decoding environment_code and cable_code.\n\nHmm wait, cable_code for models where B=1 forced... it's still B=1 → no cable. Fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe simpler to put \"environment\" directly as descriptive in model attributes and keep config_level for the mapping tables. Both is belt-and-braces. I'll do:\n\nmodels attributes: instrument_type, accuracy_class, environment_code, name_digit_B.\n\nconfig_level:\n1. attribute: \"operating_environment\", axis: \"model name letter A\", values: [{condition: \"A=S\", value: \"standard environment\"}, {condition: \"A=X\", value: \"Ex-zone 1 explosive environment\"}, {condition: \"A=Y\", value: \"Ex-zone 2 explosive environment\"}]\n   Wait text: \"denoting operation in: standard environments, Ex-zone 1 explosive environments, or Ex-zone 2 explosive environments respectively\". I'll keep plural as in text.\n2. attribute: \"rj45_cable_length\", axis: \"model name digit B\", values: [{condition: \"B=1\", value: \"no cable\"}, {condition: \"B=2\", value: 10, unit_symbol: \"m\"}, {condition: \"B=3\", value: 20, unit_symbol: \"m\"}, {condition: \"B=4\", value: 30, unit_symbol: \"m\"}]\n\nAlso the note: \"For A = S and EVO1Y models, B = 1\" — a constraint. Could add to description of config or footnote-ish. I'll note in config_level description? config_level entries don't have description per schema... they're objects with attribute, axis, values[]. I'll add a \"note\" field? Schema shows only attribute, axis, values. Keep to schema but adding extra key is probably tolerated. I'll keep strictly to schema.\n\nAlso \"C is a placeholder for commercial name affixes.\" — mention in description maybe.\n\nmatrix_tables: none in this fragment.\n\nfootnotes: none.\n\nFootnote markers: none.\n\nNow assemble JSON.\n\ncertificate:\n```json\n{\n  \"number\": \"R76/2006-A-GB1-18.07\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"page_total\": 5,\n  \"member_state\": \"United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2018-05-11\"\n}\n```\n\nissuing_authority:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"NMO\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Stanton Avenue\", \"Teddington\", \"TW11 0JZ\", \"United Kingdom\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"Mannie Panesar\",\n  \"person_title\": \"Head of Technical Services\",\n  \"phone\": \"+44 (0) 20 8943 7272\",\n  \"website\": \"www.gov.uk/government/organisations/office-for-product-safety-and-standards\"\n}\n```\n\nShould I include email? Not present. Omit.\n\napplicants:\n```json\n[{\"name\": \"Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG\", \"address_lines\": [\"37070 Goettingen\", \"Germany\"]}]\n```\n\nmanufacturers: resolved \"the applicant\":\n```json\n[{\"name\": \"Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG\", \"address_lines\": [\"37070 Goettingen\", \"Germany\"]}]\n```\n\ncertified_type:\n```json\n{\n  \"category\": \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"PMA-EV\", \"PMA-HD\", ...38 models...],\n  \"description\": \"Self-indicating, single-interval, non-automatic weighing instruments designed to be used in paint mixing applications. The family comprises the PMA-EV (Class II) and the PMA-HD (Class III) types. The instrument shall not be used for direct sales to the public. Construction: plastic construction, stainless steel load receptor, plastic display head. In the model names, letter A denotes the environment (S = standard, X = Ex-zone 1 explosive, Y = Ex-zone 2 explosive), digit B the supplied RJ-45 cable length, and C is a placeholder for commercial name affixes.\"\n}\n```\n\nmodel_family:\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": null,\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"EVO1S1N3CI-C\", \"attributes\": {\"instrument_type\": \"PMA-EV\", \"accuracy_class\": \"II\", \"environment_code\": \"S\", \"name_digit_B\": 1}},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, name_digit_B as number — from the model name directly (EVO1S1 → B=1). That's direct parsing, fine.\n\nLet me enumerate all 38 with attributes:\n\nPMA-EV, class II:\n1. EVO1S1N3CI-C: A=S, B=1\n2. EVO1S1CI-C: A=S, B=1\n3. EVO1Y1N3CI-C: A=Y, B=1\n4. EVO1Y1CI-C: A=Y, B=1\n5. EVO1X1N3CI-C: A=X, B=1\n6. EVO1X1CI-C: A=X, B=1\n7. EVO1X2N3CI-C: A=X, B=2\n8. EVO1X2CI-C: A=X, B=2\n9. EVO1X3N3CI-C: A=X, B=3\n10. EVO1X3CI-C: A=X, B=3\n11. EVO1X4N3CI-C: A=X, B=4\n12. EVO1X4CI-C: A=X, B=4\n13. VIS1Y1M3CI-C: A=Y, B=1\n14. VIS1Y1CI-C: A=Y, B=1\n15. VIS1Y2M3CI-C: A=Y, B=2\n16. VIS1Y2CI-C: A=Y, B=2\n17. VIS1Y3M3CI-C: A=Y, B=3\n18. VIS1Y3CI-C: A=Y, B=3\n19. VIS1Y4M3CI-C: A=Y, B=4\n20. VIS1Y4CI-C: A=Y, B=4\n21. VIS1X1M3CI-C: A=X, B=1\n22. VIS1X1CI-C: A=X, B=1\n23. VIS1X2M3CI-C: A=X, B=2\n24. VIS1X2CI-C: A=X, B=2\n25. VIS1X3M3CI-C: A=X, B=3\n26. VIS1X3CI-C: A=X, B=3\n27. VIS1X4M3CI-C: A=X, B=4\n28. VIS1X4CI-C: A=X, B=4\n\nWait, but the text says \"For A = S and EVO1Y models, B = 1 and for all other models B = 1, 2, 3, or 4\". Yet VIS1Y2M3CI-C has B=2 with A=Y. The text says EVO1Y specifically (not VIS1Y). Odd but fine — VIS1Y models do have B=2,3,4 in the list. So per-model B from the name is accurate regardless.\n\nPMA-HD, class III:\n29. SPO1S1N3CI-C: A=S, B=1\n30. SPO1S1CI-C: A=S, B=1\n31. LAB1X1N3CI-C: A=X, B=1\n32. LAB1X1CI-C: A=X, B=1\n33. LAB1X2N3CI-C: A=X, B=2\n34. LAB1X2CI-C: A=X, B=2\n35. LAB1X3N3CI-C: A=X, B=3\n36. LAB1X3CI-C: A=X, B=3\n37. LAB1X4N3CI-C: A=X, B=4\n38. LAB1X4CI-C: A=X, B=4\n\nOK.\n\ncharacteristics:\n- type_level: maybe include \"accuracy_class\"? That's in recommendation. Skip type_level? Or include construction features? I'll skip type_level since no clean numeric/structured attribute at cert level... Actually \"intervals\": \"single-interval\" and \"indicator\": \"self-indicating\" — description covers it. Skip.\n\n- model_level: none numeric (no capacities given in this fragment — page 4 likely has them but not in fragment). So no model_level.\n\n- config_level: the two axes above.\n\nrecommendation:\n```json\n{\"id\": \"R76\", \"edition\": 2006, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"II\", \"III\"]}\n```\n\ntest_reports:\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\": \"P02276\", \"date\": \"2018-05-11\", \"pages\": 30, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"P02276-D\", \"date\": \"2018-05-11\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n```\n\nrevision_history:\n```json\n[{\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2018-05-11\", \"changes\": \"OIML Certificate first issued.\"}]\n```\n\nfootnotes: [] — omit (not present). Actually \"Footnote-like\" notes? No.\n\nOutput JSON only. Include certificate.issuing_authority.oiml_issuer_id? Not present; omit.